/* book Worked example 2: Q: A stepped cantilever is 2 m long and carries P = 10 kN at its free end. The inner half (0 to 1 m from the fixed end) has EI = 4000 kN·m²; the outer half (1 to 2 m) has EI = 2000 kN·m². Find the tip deflection and compare it with uniform beams of each stiffness.
   1. x from the fixed end. Real moment M = −P(2 − x) = −10(2 − x) kN·m; unit load 1 down at the tip gives m = −(2 − x). So Mm = 10(2 − x)² on both segments.
   2. Segment 1, 0 to 1 m: ∫ 10(2 − x)² dx = 10 × [(2)³ − (1)³]/3 = 10 × 7/3 = 23.33 kN·m³. Divide by 4000: 5.833 × 10⁻³ m.
   3. Segment 2, 1 to 2 m: ∫ 10(2 − x)² dx = 10 × (1)³/3 = 3.333 kN·m³. Divide by 2000: 1.667 × 10⁻³ m.
   4. Δ = 5.833 + 1.667 = 7.50 mm downward.
   5. Uniform beam at EI = 2000: PL³/(3EI) = 10 × 8/6000 = 13.33 mm. Uniform at 4000: 6.67 mm.
   Answer: Tip deflection 7.50 mm. The stiff root half carries 78 % of the integral, so the beam behaves almost like the uniform 4000 kN·m² beam: stiffness near the root matters most, which is why wing spars are deepest there. */
