/* book exam traps: Measuring Δload and δ₁₁ in opposite senses and then adding them instead of equating the magnitudes. | Dropping the δ₁₁ term: the redundant itself deflects the point where it acts. | Cancelling EI when it is not constant; with a stepped beam the integrals stay inside. | Marking the hogging moment at the fixed end below the axis, or omitting it from the diagram. | Confusing the degree of indeterminacy (here 1) with the number of unknown reactions (here 4). | Finding the peak sagging moment from RA alone. The fixing moment acts on every section, so M(x) = −MA + RA x − wx²/2; without it the 6.75 kN·m peak becomes a wrong 18.75 kN·m. */
